# Writing CATS Tests

You are helping a user author pytest tests against deployed CATS infrastructure.
The fastest path to a good test is to probe reality first, then codify what you
confirmed as an assertion. MCP tools and `cats run` subcommands are made for
that probe loop — use them before writing code.

## Probe before you write

If you have the CATS MCP server connected, use its tools. Otherwise, the same
surface is available from any shell via `cats run`. Both return JSON.

```bash
cats run account-info --account myaccount
cats run tcp-call     --account myaccount --target private --host example.com --port 443
cats run http-call    --account myaccount --target private --host 10.0.1.5    --port 8080
cats run tcp-call     --account myaccount --target private --host 10.0.1.5    --port 22
cats run udp-call     --account myaccount --target private --host <other listener dns> --port 5000 --receiving-account other
cats run receive-s3   --account other     --target private --correlation-id <uuid>
```

See the [CLI reference](https://awslabs.github.io/cats/api-reference/CLI.html) and [MCP reference](https://awslabs.github.io/cats/api-reference/MCP.html) for full details, or `cats prompts agentic` for the in-agent tool reference. Once you know what
the infrastructure actually does, the pytest test writes itself.

## Basic test structure

```python
from cats import AccountUnderTest, CatsNetworkResponse

def test_example_com_443_reachable(myaccount_account: AccountUnderTest) -> None:
    """Private subnet can open a TCP connection to example.com on 443."""
    result = myaccount_account.private.tcp_call(host="example.com", port=443)
    assert result == CatsNetworkResponse.SUCCESS
```

Match the method to the claim. `tcp_call` proves the path is open. `http_call` sends a
plaintext `GET /`, so point it (and `cats run http-call`) at a port that speaks plain HTTP,
such as a CATS listener's `tcp_listen_ports`: against a TLS port it returns `FAILURE` or
`REFUSED`, never `SUCCESS`. `https_call` proves the TLS handshake and an HTTP answer, and is
the only method that can return `TLS_FAILURE`.

## Available fixtures

Each account defined in `cats.yaml` becomes a fixture named `<keyname>_account`.
Run `cats generate-types` after every config change so your IDE sees them.

## Typing rule

No generated types → annotate fixtures as `AccountUnderTest`. Generated types
(`cats generate-types` produces `cats_types.py`) → use the protocols everywhere:
`<Keyname>Account` for a specific account, `CatsAccountProtocol` for a helper
that accepts any account. Do not mix the two — passing a protocol-typed fixture
to an `AccountUnderTest`-typed function does not type-check.

```python
from cats_types import CatsAccountProtocol, MyaccountAccount

def assert_no_public_buckets(account: CatsAccountProtocol) -> None:
    """Helper accepting any account."""
    ...

def test_no_public_buckets(myaccount_account: MyaccountAccount) -> None:
    assert_no_public_buckets(myaccount_account)
```

For dynamic network target access by name, use `getattr(account, target_name)`.

Network targets appear as attributes on the fixture, matching
`account_network_under_test` keys. Each returns a `NetworkHandler` with:

- `.http_call(host, port)` / `.https_call(...)`
- `.tcp_call(host, port)`
- `.udp_call(host, port)`
- `.receive_s3(sent)` on the receiving side, where `sent` is the result of a
  `udp_call`. UDP ingestion is authenticated: CATS listeners drop datagrams
  lacking a per-account receipt token, which `udp_call` prepends automatically —
  never build the datagram yourself, and expect `receive_s3` to return `False`
  for raw untokened sends.
- `.shell(command)` — arbitrary shell command on the target EC2 instance; requires `allow_shell: true` on `account_settings` at deploy time. Runs as root with the EC2 instance's IAM role, not the CATS test role. Returns `ShellResult(stdout, stderr, exit_code, duration_ms, stdout_truncated, stderr_truncated)`. Use when http/tcp/udp aren't enough — `dig`, `traceroute`, `ip route`, `ss`, custom `curl` flags.

Every network call returns a `CatsNetworkResult`. Compare it to a
`CatsNetworkResponse` member with `==`; never with `is`, and never to a bare
string (`result == "TIMEOUT"` is always `False`). The result also carries
`reason` (the probe's error text), `http_status` (HTTP/HTTPS `SUCCESS` only),
and `correlation_id` (UDP only). None of those take part in equality, and all of
them are printed when an assertion fails, so a failing test explains itself.

### UDP example

```python
def test_udp_reaches_listener(prod_account: AccountUnderTest) -> None:
    sent = prod_account.private.udp_call(prod_account.private.ec2_dns_name, 5000)
    assert sent == CatsNetworkResponse.SENT
    assert prod_account.private.receive_s3(sent) is True
```

### `shell()` example

```python
def test_dns_resolves(prod_account: AccountUnderTest) -> None:
    result = prod_account.private.shell("dig +short example.com")
    assert result.exit_code == 0
```

## Test markers

Use the `@cats` decorator to attach compliance standards and other metadata.
Markers flow through to `report.json` and, when `reporting.report_formats`
includes `oscal_ar`, into `assessment-results.json` as OSCAL observation
properties and findings. For compliance workflows that need framework control
IDs (`AC-2`, `CC6.1`, etc.) rather than the raw `standard_name` string, either
pass `control_ids=[...]` directly to the decorator for per-test overrides, or
implement the `pytest_cats_standards` pytest hook in the consuming repo's
`conftest.py` to return a registry mapping your `standard_name` values to
framework control IDs.

```python
from cats import cats

@cats(standard_name="iso-1234")
def test_compliance_requirement(myaccount_account: AccountUnderTest) -> None:
    ...
```

`@cats` is optional: every test is recorded in `report.json` whether or not it is
decorated, and only tests carrying a `standard_name` become compliance findings.
Its keywords are exactly `standard_name`, `control_ids` and `control_group`; any
other keyword is a custom marker that must be listed under
`reporting.custom_markers` in cats.yaml, or the import raises `CatsUsageError`.
Skipping is pytest's job (`@pytest.mark.skip`), not a `@cats` keyword.

## Which tests run against this deployment

Two facts decide it, and they live in different places because they are different
kinds of knowledge:

- **Structural** — which deployments a test applies to. Whoever writes the test knows
  this, and declares it on the test with `@cats_requires(...)`.
- **Situational** — a property of this environment or this policy. That belongs in
  `test_selection` of cats.yaml, with a reason.

```python
from cats import cats, cats_requires

@cats_requires("container-deployment")
@cats(standard_name="ACME-C-DEP-1")
def test_app_runs_as_ecs_task(myaccount_account: AccountUnderTest) -> None:
    """Applies only to a container deployment."""

@cats_requires("pipeline-deployment", "container-deployment")   # both must be provided
@cats(standard_name="ACME-PIPE-1")
def test_pipeline_signs_container_images(myaccount_account: AccountUnderTest) -> None:
    """Applies only to the interaction of the two variants."""
```

```yaml
reporting:
  custom_markers: [mutating]

test_selection:
  provides: [container-deployment]        # what this deployment satisfies
  excluded:
    - marker: standard_name               # standard_name, cats_requires, or a declared custom marker
      values: [ACME-G-SCP-34]
      reason: "RDS not in use; risk accepted by the CISO"
    - marker: mutating
      values: [true]                      # a YAML boolean matches @cats(mutating=True); "true" does not
      reason: "Change freeze; read-only run"
```

The rules:

- A test runs only when **every** requirement it declares is provided (AND, no OR, no
  negation). Names are free-form strings; a new variant is a new name. Mutually exclusive
  variants are two positive requirements (`standard-deployment` / `container-deployment`).
  A test with no `@cats_requires` always runs.
- `provides` is unioned with what the test pack's `pytest_cats_provides` hook returns,
  so a pack can provide its defaults from its conftest.
- `excluded` holds a test back when any entry matches one of its marker values. The
  `marker` must be `standard_name`, `cats_requires` or a name in `reporting.custom_markers`:
  a typo is a `cats validate` error, never an exclusion that silently matches nothing.
- Every held-back test is in the reports as `not_run` with its reason: the exclusion's
  `reason`, or `requires {a, b}; not provided {b}`. A test `-k`, `-m` or `--deselect`
  removed is **not** in the reports at all. An `excluded` entry that matched no test is named in the
  terminal summary and `run.selection.unmatched_exclusions`.
- Providing a requirement only ever runs more tests. It never relaxes an assertion, and
  tests cannot read the provided set: it is a precondition, not a feature flag. If a
  control behaves differently in a variant, write two tests with two standard names.

When adapting a test pack you have adopted: a test you add that applies only to some
deployments gets `@cats_requires`; a control you opt out of gets an `excluded` entry
with the reason, which keeps it in the evidence and lives in your config, so it survives
taking upstream changes to the pack; delete the test when you want neither the control
nor a record of it.

## Parametrizing across every account

To assert the same thing against every configured account, parametrize on the
generated fixture names:

```python
import pytest
from cats import CatsNetworkResponse, GetAccountFixture, cats, get_account_fixture_names

@cats(standard_name="no-ssh-port")
@pytest.mark.parametrize("fixturename", get_account_fixture_names())
def test_ssh_closed(fixturename: str, get_account_fixture: GetAccountFixture) -> None:
    account = get_account_fixture(fixturename)
    result = account.private.tcp_call(host=account.private.ec2_dns_name, port=22)
    # REFUSED: nothing listens on 22 (sshd is disabled on the CATS listener), and this
    # self-call never leaves the host so security groups don't apply. From any other
    # host (e.g. the lambda handler) an unlisted port returns TIMEOUT instead: the
    # listener's CATS-managed group only admits configured tcp/udp_listen_ports.
    assert result == CatsNetworkResponse.REFUSED
```

## Network response semantics

Assert the exact outcome, not just "not SUCCESS". Each value is a precise claim
about what the probing endpoint observed; which control produced it is your
interpretation, and belongs in the test docstring.

- `SUCCESS` - connection established. Any HTTP response (including 3xx/4xx/5xx;
  redirects are not followed) is SUCCESS. `result.http_status` carries the code:
  assert on it when a proxy block page (403/407/451) must be told apart from the
  real target.
- `REFUSED` - RST received (refused or reset): the path is OPEN, nothing accepted.
  For HTTP, a listener that accepted then closed without any response is also
  REFUSED. If you expected traffic to be blocked, REFUSED means the control is NOT
  working. A Network Firewall REJECT looks the same as nothing listening.
- `TIMEOUT` - the TCP connect got no answer, consistent with a silent drop:
  the expected evidence for "traffic is blocked" (SG, NACL, Network Firewall
  DROP, route table, data perimeter). All silent drops look identical from the
  endpoint, and transient loss is indistinguishable from enforcement.
- `DNS_FAILURE` - the name did not resolve (NXDOMAIN/NODATA, or the resolver was
  unreachable). What a Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall block produces, and also
  what a typo produces. No packet was ever sent to the target.
- `TLS_FAILURE` - TCP connected, the TLS handshake failed: untrusted or mismatched
  certificate, a plaintext listener on the port, protocol mismatch. What TLS
  inspection with an untrusted CA produces. `https_call` only; the OpenSSL text
  is in `result.reason`.
- `FAILURE` - the path could not be proven open or blocked: a connect that
  succeeded with no HTTP reply inside the budget (a hung backend, NOT a silent
  drop; `result.reason` starts "connected; no HTTP reply"), or an unclassified
  error (`ENETUNREACH`, `EHOSTUNREACH`, ...; the errno is in `result.reason`).
  Never an expected value.
- `SENT` - UDP only: the datagram left the host. Prove arrival with
  `receive_s3(sent)`. UDP can also return `DNS_FAILURE` or `FAILURE`, never
  `REFUSED` or `TIMEOUT`.

### Denial tests

A negative (isolation) test needs a known-ready listener at the target and an
allowed-path positive control in the same run, otherwise a broken listener passes
as "blocked". `TIMEOUT` is the only outcome consistent with a silent DROP.
`REFUSED` means the path is open. `DNS_FAILURE` means the control that acted was
the resolver, which is a different claim from "packets were dropped": assert it
as `DNS_FAILURE`, not as a stand-in for `TIMEOUT`.

## Running tests

```bash
cats test                    # Run with defaults (-vv -ra)
cats test -k "test_name"     # Run specific test
cats test --collect-only     # List tests without running
```

## Idiomatic rules

- Write one assertion against a concrete expected value. No `in` checks, no
  `startswith` — expected value, then `assert expected == actual`.
- Name tests `test_<condition>_<expected_outcome>`.
- If probing via MCP or `cats run` returned something unexpected, write the
  failing test first to pin the bug, then fix.
- Leave infrastructure ephemeral. Deploy, test, destroy — always.
